<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Justanid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">791 CE – 1004 CE (approx.) · capital Rudbar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Daylamite royal family in the valleys of the Alborz that sheltered Alid refugees and is credited with building the castle of Alamut.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Justanids were rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, the highlands south-west of the Caspian, with their centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudbar-of-alamut">Rudbar</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrud-river">Shahrud</a>. They first appear in 791, when King Marzuban ibn Justan gave refuge to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alid</a> rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-abdallah">Yahya ibn Abdallah</a>, before handing him over to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a> were tough highland infantry who long resisted Arab rule. The Justanids governed through clan loyalties and accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 9th century, often in its Zaydi Shia form under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alid</a> missionaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justan-iii">Justan III</a> supported the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alavid-dynasty">Alavid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> after 864, and tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahsudan-ibn-marzuban">Wahsudan ibn Marzuban</a> with building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> castle in about 860.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the early 10th century the Justanids were overshadowed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sallarid-dynasty">Sallarid dynasty</a>, who married into their family and took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>. The family lingered as minor lords and disappears from the sources around the early 11th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 791 CE – 1004 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.44%;width:4.24%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>791 CE – 1004 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rudbar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marzuban ibn Justan, Wahsudan ibn Marzuban, Justan III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, later Zaydi Shia</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Daylami</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Justanids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=898&amp;focus=justanid-dynasty" aria-label="Justanid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5920 851 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Justanid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6240 1107L6202 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6202 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6240 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6240 1107h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rudbar. <a href="/atlas?year=898&amp;focus=justanid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

